FreeSDN event automation coordination
===================================

Event-triggered actions require successful shared coordination. A failed,
unavailable, malformed or uncertain Redis/Valkey response never permits a local
fallback. This covers the event claim, configured cooldown and hourly rate limit.
Only the actual Redis SET acknowledgement (True) grants a claim/cooldown; only
integer 1 from the atomic rate-limit script grants an execution. Integer 0 is a
normal rate-limit refusal. Distinct attempts with identical timestamps occupy
distinct rate-limit entries.

This boundary matters for camera actions as well as notifications. Native camera
handlers do not all use the Fabric staged-write path. An uncertain event must
not be allowed merely because another action family has its own write gate.

Operator observations
---------------------

GET /api/v1/automation/summary includes coordination_blocked_this_worker for the
caller's organization. Each entry contains a rule ID, a fixed reason and the
first observation time for that reason. It contains no event payload or client
connection details. These refusals do not increment trigger_count or fabricate a
successful execution. A subsequent definite coordination decision clears the
observation; pausing or removing the rule on that worker also clears it.

This is process-local diagnostic state, not durable audit history or a complete
fleet view. A request routed to a different worker can return different entries;
an empty list does not prove every worker or rule is healthy. The web rule cards
do not yet display these diagnostics. Review the API and application warnings.

Recovery boundaries
-------------------

A timeout may arrive after the shared store has accepted the claim. The engine
does not delete that claim or a reserved cooldown after a later failure. An event
may therefore be missed. There is no automatic replay or catch-up after outage.
Reconcile effects before any deliberate manual retry, especially camera actions
and external notifications. Manual Run Now retains its existing explicit path;
these new coordination checks govern event-driven execution.

The event claim lasts 300 seconds. A shared event ID suppresses duplicates only
while its claim remains present. Expiry, eviction, store loss, restoring an old
snapshot, clock skew and an action lasting beyond its claim are not exactly-once
guarantees. Callers omitting event_id receive rate/cooldown checks without an
event-identity claim. External effects still require idempotency and coordinated
restore/fencing. This change does not qualify hardware operations, repair stale
rule caches across workers or implement a durable event inbox/outbox.

Local rule subscription replacement
-----------------------------------
Re-registering the same rule ID replaces its event-pattern entries under the
engine lock. Previous patterns and duplicates are removed, including all legacy
duplicates on deletion. Fixing an empty pattern clears its local diagnostic, and
changing an event rule to a manual trigger removes its event subscription. Rule
edits refresh this local index after the database flush succeeds.

This does not synchronize the other workers' rule caches or make their mutation
transactional with a later database commit/rollback. An operator disable/edit
must not be described as a proven fleet-wide execution fence on the strength of
this local fix. That behavior needs its own persisted-state/fencing design and
multi-process qualification.
